  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of early this morning, a deepening \nNor'easter was located about 230 miles NE of Cape Hatteras, and \nwas moving slowly north. Guidance is now well-clustered with the\ntrack of this low as it continues north towards Southern New \nEngland. However, the northward progress should be slowed by an \nupper ridge sliding east from the Great Lakes to Northern New \nEngland over the weekend. This should steer the Nor'easter west \ntowards the Northern Mid-Atlantic later tonight into Sunday. \nEventually, the system will weaken and lift NE away from the \nU.S. East Coast.\n\nOn the southern periphery of the Nor'easter, a moderate to \nstrong gradient is forecast to remain in place across northeast \nNC and the northern OBX. Despite a bit of a lull in the \nstrongest winds this morning, the wind forecast was left mostly \nunchanged as guidance suggests the gradient may restrengthen as \nthe Nor'easter potentially moves a bit west of due north along \nthe coast. It's possible that the strongest gradient, and \nstrongest winds, will remain just east of the coast, and \nadjustments down with the wind forecast may be needed. It should\nbe noted that the gradient is expected to be significantly \nweaker across the southwestern half of our forecast area, with \nnoticeably wind differences from NE to SW. That said, a dry and \nwell-mixed airmass today should support one more day of gusty \nwinds of 20-25mph inland away from the coast.\n\nThe dry airmass should keep most of ENC dry today. The one \nexception, though, is along the NRN OBX. There, increasing CAA \non the western periphery of the Nor'easter should lead to steep \nlow- level lapse rates along the length of the Chesapeake Bay \n(where water temps are still in the 70s). This plus a favorable \ntrajectory down the length of the Bay should support a band of \nshowers extending southeast from eastern VA into NE NC. \n\nDespite winds gradually decreasing today, coastal impacts will \ncontinue to plague portions of the OBX. Buoys currently show \nwave heights of 10-13ft at 12-13 seconds, and this should \ncontinue to lead to wave runup and ocean overwash impacts along \nvulnerable stretches of the OBX, with a continued focus on \nHatteras and Ocracoke Islands. Portions of NC-12 remain closed \non both Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands. Adding to the impact of \nthe wave runup and overwash is the already elevated astronomical\ntides ongoing through the weekend. A High Surf Advisory remains\nin effect until this evening for those areas. For soundside \nlocations, water levels remain the most elevated for areas \nfavored in north or northwest flow, including areas around \nOcracoke and Hatteras Villages. Various coastal flood headlines \nremain in effect through the morning high tide, but may need to \nbe extended in time depending on how the winds and seas evolve \ntoday. In addition, as the Nor'easter pulls away from the area, \na more dominant westerly wind is expected to develop by Sunday, \nand this may lead to a risk of minor coastal flooding around \nRoanoke Island.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...By mid to late next week, a notable upper level\nridge is forecast to build over the Southeastern U.S. This \ncombined with rising low-level thicknesses in southerly flow \nshould support warming temperatures, including the potential for\na period of above normal highs once again. Moisture is forecast\nto be slower to return, and this should support mostly dry \nconditions for much of the upcoming week.",
